  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWith a strong ridge of high pressure to our south we have \npredominantly zonal flow aloft. While tonight into tomorrow was\noriginally looking drier, water vapor and satellite imagery is \nshowing a stronger shortwave moving in late this afternoon and \novernight. CAMs have picked up on more shower activity so \nchances of precipitation were increased overnight. With the \nincrease in low level moisture and continued warm air advection \ntomorrow, precipitation chances still look good with a weak \nfrontal passage Thursday morning into the afternoon. \nPrecipitable water values creep above 1.5 inches so any \nthunderstorms or showers that do form along the front will have \nheavy rain. Timing of the front is early in the day prior to any\nsignificant CAPE development but if the front can trend later, \nthere could be a low severe thunderstorm risk with greater than \n30 knots of shear in place.\n\nSurface high pressure builds back in Friday with drier weather \nreturning for Friday into Saturday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nLate this weekend into early next week the center of the ridge \nto our south moves off the SE coast with some hints at a weak \nsurface low forming in the gulf. This will help with moisture \nreturning to our region. With the Zonal flow aloft and a trough \ntrying to dig into the Northeast US, a convergence zone/stalled \nfrontal boundary will form from the Great Lakes into the Mid \nAtlantic. While the location of this stalled boundary is still \nuncertain, many of the models and ensembles have ticked upwards \nwith their accumulated precipitation amounts. Deterministic and \nindividual ensemble members are consistent with up to 4 inches \nof rain over several days where this convergence zone eventually\nsets up.",
